        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Systems Engineering - Landscape and Taxonomy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "**Bridge to software**: Systems engineering is software architecture's ancestor. The SE V-model maps directly onto SDLC: requirements -> design -> implementation -> unit test -> integration test -> system test -> acceptance test. The test pyramid is the V-model applied to software with physical verification replaced by automated test suites. FMEA (failure mode and effects analysis) is the physical-system equivalent of distributed systems failure analysis: for each component, what happens if it fails? How does that cascade? Is there a SPOF? The \"local effect -> next-higher effect -> end effect\" chain in FMEA maps to \"service failure -> dependency cascade -> user-visible impact\" in microservices. Architecture decision records (ADRs), interface specifications, requirements traceability — all have direct software equivalents. The tools differ but the intellectual framework is the same."
    },
